---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';
import Limits from '../components/Limits.astro';
import { REPO_URL } from '../site';
---

<Base
  fullTitle="Free, private Outlook alternative for Windows and Mac · OpenWren"
  description="OpenWren is a free, open-source Outlook alternative for Gmail and IMAP accounts: no ads, mail kept on your own computer, and a search that finds things."
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <h1>A free, private Outlook alternative for Windows and Mac</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      If you use Outlook for a Gmail account or a mailbox from your internet or hosting provider, you are carrying a
      large program for a small job. OpenWren is a free, open-source email client that does that job: your mail, on
      your computer, with a search that works. It is not a replacement for Outlook with Microsoft 365, and this page
      says so where it matters.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/download/">Download OpenWren</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/search/">How search works</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <Screenshot src="inbox.png" alt="The OpenWren inbox: folders, the message list and an open message" label="Inbox" width={760} />

    <h2>Why people leave Outlook</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Search.</strong> A message you can see in the list does not come up when you search for it. It is the complaint people repeat most about Outlook, year after year.</li>
      <li><strong>Ads and a subscription.</strong> The free version of the new Outlook shows ads in the message list; removing them costs a Microsoft 365 subscription.</li>
      <li><strong>Where your mail goes.</strong> When you add a Gmail or IMAP account to the new Outlook, Microsoft keeps a copy of that mail on its own servers.</li>
      <li><strong>Size.</strong> It is a calendar, a task list, a contact manager and a mail program, whether you use them or not.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Outlook and OpenWren side by side</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>OpenWren</th><th>New Outlook</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Price</td><td>Free, no paid version</td><td>Free with ads, or a Microsoft 365 subscription</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Open source</td><td>Yes (Apache 2.0)</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gmail and IMAP accounts</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Microsoft 365, Exchange, Outlook.com</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Where Gmail and IMAP mail is kept</td><td>On your computer, encrypted</td><td>Also copied to Microsoft's servers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Search</td><td>Own index on your computer, all accounts at once, works offline</td><td>Mostly done by Microsoft's servers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tracking pixels and link trackers</td><td>Removed</td><td>Images can be blocked; link trackers stay</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Calendar, contacts, tasks</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Needs an account with the maker</td><td>No</td><td>A Microsoft account</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Linux</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">
      To the best of my knowledge in November 2026. If a cell is wrong or out of date, please
      <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>say so</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Stay with Outlook if</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>your mail is a Microsoft 365 or Exchange account at work, or an Outlook.com or Hotmail address;</li>
      <li>you live in the calendar, share calendars or book meeting rooms;</li>
      <li>your company's IT department manages your mail program.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try OpenWren if</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>your accounts are Gmail or IMAP (most providers and hosting companies);</li>
      <li>you want all of them in one inbox, with <a href="/search/">one search box</a> that answers in milliseconds;</li>
      <li>you would rather your mail stayed on your own computer, and want to be able to check that it does: the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> lists every network request the app makes;</li>
      <li>you want mail only, in a 14 MB download.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What OpenWren does not do</h2>
    <Limits />

    <h2>Moving over</h2>
    <p>
      There is nothing to export. With IMAP your mail lives on your mail server, so you add the same account in
      OpenWren and it downloads your folders and messages. Keep Outlook installed until you are happy; both can use
      the same account at once. Start on the page for <a href="/windows/">Windows</a> or <a href="/mac/">Mac</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only looking to repair Outlook's search? See
      <a href="/outlook-search-not-working/">Outlook search not working: how to fix it</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
